  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will build eastward across the area today bringing\nsunny and seasonable weather to the area. Lows tonight will fall\nback into the 30s and 40s. No frost headlines are expected in\nthe continued growing season areas with lows mainly in the upper\n30s or 40s. This high will begin to depart to the east on \nSunday as an upper level shortwave approaches from the west and\nweak area of low pressure tracks off the East Coast. These\nfeatures will allow for an increase in clouds in the afternoon\nand a low chance for a few showers in spots, especially for \nareas south and east of Albany.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA potent upper level and surface low will be slowly tracking\nacross Quebec early next week. This feature will send a strong\ncold front across the area on Monday. This front will pick up\nenough moisture from the Great Lakes for a few showers mainly\nfor areas north and west of Albany later Sunday night into\nMonday. Strong cold air advection then takes place behind the \nfront as 850 hPa temperatures fall to near or below 0C. Tuesday \nwill be the coolest day of the week with highs mainly in the 50s\nexcept upper 40s across the Adirondacks and southern Greens and\nlower 60s in the mid-Hudson Valley. It will be breezy on Monday\nwith winds gusting 20 to 30 mph if not locally higher across \nthe Mohawk Valley, Capital District and Berkshires. While lows\nMonday night fall back into the 30s to lower 40s, there should\nbe enough wind to prevent frost. The better frost night would be\nTuesday night with light to calm winds with the high directly\noverhead. Frost Advisories may be needed for some areas where\nthe growing season is still active. Temperatures rebound back\ntoward normal values for the second half of the week with the\nnext chance for some rain showers being during this time as\nwell as another upper level trough approaches.",
